# Battle Targeting System

## Core Targeting Principles [to-do]
- **[PRIORITY]** Implement proximity-based targeting (closest valid target)
- Apply universal targeting rules across all battalion types
- Restrict movement and targeting to network lines
- Ensure targeting aligns with pathfinding system
- Focus exclusively on targeting nodes 3, 4, and 5 for node capture

## Initial Target Selection [to-do]
- At battle start, battalions randomly select one accessible node from nodes 3, 4, and 5
- Determine valid paths to target nodes via network connections
- Only target nodes that can be reached via connected network lines
- Calculate initial movement paths to selected target
- Handle cases where multiple battalions target the same node

## Target Selection Rules [to-do]
- Target nearest available entity (enemy battalion or node)
- Calculate distance from battalion center to target center along network lines
- Integrate with A* pathfinding from movement system
- Prioritize nodes 3, 4, and 5 for targeting
- Consider battalion and node positions in real-time

## Dynamic Retargeting System [to-do]
- While moving, continuously check for closer available targets
- Once attacking, maintain current target until:
  1. Target is destroyed/captured
  2. Target moves out of range
  3. Battalion itself is defeated
- Prioritize closest target when retargeting (measured along network lines)
- Ensure seamless transition between targets
- Recalculate paths when retargeting occurs

## Combat Range [to-do]
- Define attack range values for each battalion type based on Digital Barracks stats
- Calculate attack range from battalion center to target center
- Determine when battalions are in range to attack
- Stop movement when attack range reaches target
- Maintain visual indicators for attack range

## Combat Sequence [to-do]
- Verify target is in range before initiating attack
- Implement basic damage calculation logic
- Update health status after attacks
- Trigger appropriate visual feedback
- Tie attack frequency to battalion speed stat

## MVP Implementation Focus
- Prioritize targeting restrictions to nodes 3, 4, and 5
- Implement proximity-based targeting along network lines
- Ensure proper dynamic retargeting while moving
- Maintain target lock once attacking begins
- Optimize for responsive targeting and smooth gameplay

The MVP implementation should focus on creating a reliable proximity-based targeting system that works within network line restrictions. Advanced features like complex pathfinding and special targeting rules can be added in future iterations. 